{
  "id": "astro-002",
  "title": "Why do we have day and night?",
  "category": "astronomy",
  "subject_area": "Earth's rotation",
  "difficulty_level": "introductory",
  "target_audience": [
    "general public",
    "primary school",
    "secondary school"
  ],
  "central_question": "Why does the Sun seem to rise and set every day?",
  "template": "principle",
  "principle_statement": "Earth spins on its axis once every 24 hours, so each place on Earth turns toward the Sun for day and away from it for night.",
  "domain_of_validity": "Earth's daily rotation as seen from the ground; near the poles, day and night also depend on the time of year.",
  "scientific_explanation": {
    "level_1": "Earth is spinning like a top, and one full spin takes about 24 hours. The Sun always lights up half of Earth. The place where you live turns toward the Sun, and it is day. Hours later it has turned away from the Sun, and it is night. The Sun is not really moving across the sky. You are moving with the spinning Earth.",
    "level_2": "Earth rotates around its axis, an imaginary line through the North and South Poles, once every 24 hours. At any moment half of Earth is lit by the Sun and half is in darkness. Places facing the Sun have daytime, and places facing away have nighttime. Because Earth spins, the Sun appears to move across the sky from east to west. Near the North and South Poles there can be continuous daylight or darkness, depending on the time of year.",
    "level_3": "At the equator Earth's surface moves at about 2,200 kilometres per hour, which is just over 1,000 miles per hour. It covers a distance d equal to Earth's circumference in a time t of 24 hours, so v = d / t. We cannot feel this motion. A globe lit by a flashlight shows the same effect: as the globe turns, a point on it passes from the lit side to the dark side and back."
  },
  "supporting_evidence": [
    "Shine a flashlight on a globe and spin it slowly: a spot on the globe moves from light to dark and back again.",
    "At the equator Earth spins at about 2,200 kilometres per hour, yet we cannot feel it."
  ],
